6.What is Ingress?
Ingress manages external HTTP/HTTPS traffic and routes it to Services.
For example, suppose you have:


example.com
api.example.com

You can route them to different Services:

                  Ingress
                     │
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          ↓                     ↓
    / → frontend-service    /api → backend-service
          ↓                     ↓
       Frontend Pods         Backend Pods


A basic Ingress:
ingress.yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress

metadata:
  name: nginx-ingress

spec:
  rules:
    - host: nginx.example.com

      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix

            backend:
              service:
                name: nginx-service
                port:
                  number: 80




spec:
  rules:
This is where you define your routing rules.

- host: nginx.example.com
This means:   If a request comes for nginx.example.com, use the following routing rule.


http:    This says we are defining HTTP routing.



paths:
  - path: /
This means requests coming to:  nginx.example.com/
should follow this rule.
For example:
nginx.example.com/
nginx.example.com/about
nginx.example.com/products




pathType: Prefix, it means:   Match the beginning (prefix) of the URL path.
For example:
- path: /cart
  pathType: Prefix

This matches:
/cart
/cart/items
/cart/123
/cart/checkout
because they all start with /cart.

But it won't match unrelated paths like:
/buy
/product


/ is the beginning of every URL path, so it can match:
 /
 /cart
 /buy
 /product
 /anything

backend
It means:   Send this request to the Service named nginx-service on port 80.




For example, suppose you have:

/cart
/buy
/product

and each path should go to a different Service.

You can put all of them in one ingress.yaml:

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress

metadata:
  name: shopping-ingress

spec:
  rules:
    - host: shop.example.com

      http:
        paths:

          - path: /cart
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: cart-service
                port:
                  number: 80

          - path: /buy
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: buy-service
                port:
                  number: 80

          - path: /product
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: product-service
                port:
                  number: 80

Why use one Ingress?
Because Ingress can contain many routing rules.
You could also create separate Ingress objects, but for a simple application, keeping related routes in one Ingress is usually easier.
Remember:    One Ingress can route many paths → many Services → many Pods.


